WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.760 --> 00:00:05.680
Welcome to our short course in&nbsp;
economics where we learn how we&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:05.680 --> 00:00:11.520
make choices when unlimited human wants&nbsp;
meet limited resources. Supply and demand&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:11.520 --> 00:00:16.400
shows how people coordinate their decisions&nbsp;
by communicating through prices. “Supply”&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:16.400 --> 00:00:20.320
refers to the number of goods and services&nbsp;
that sellers produce at a certain price.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:20.960 --> 00:00:25.520
“Demand” refers to how many goods and services&nbsp;
consumers are willing to buy at a certain price.

00:00:28.080 --> 00:00:32.400
Let’s assume there is just one coffee shop&nbsp;
on the market, but 100 people who drink&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:32.400 --> 00:00:38.320
coffee. The price of coffee is $3 per cup,&nbsp;
and the people each drink one cup per day.

00:00:40.720 --> 00:00:44.960
We can illustrate this with a diagram&nbsp;
showing the price per cup of coffee on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:44.960 --> 00:00:49.760
the vertical axis and the number of cups&nbsp;
of coffee consumed on the horizontal axis.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:50.400 --> 00:00:56.160
At $3, 100 cups are demanded. What&nbsp;
do you think, how many cups of coffee&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:56.160 --> 00:01:00.480
would these 100 people drink if the&nbsp;
price of a cup were to increase to $8?

00:01:03.520 --> 00:01:10.400
While at $3, a 100 people drink a cup of&nbsp;
coffee, at $8, maybe just 30 do. And at $12,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:11.040 --> 00:01:14.560
there could be as few as 10 people&nbsp;
interested in purchasing a cup.&nbsp;

00:01:14.560 --> 00:01:22.400
However, at $1 per cup, demand could go up to 150&nbsp;
cups a day, because some of those original 100&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:22.400 --> 00:01:28.480
customers are now drinking two or more cups.
This is what economists call a demand curve.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:28.480 --> 00:01:34.160
It shows how much quantity is in demand at&nbsp;
each price. The curve usually slopes downward&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:34.160 --> 00:01:39.840
because economists assume that the lower&nbsp;
the price the higher the quantity demanded.

00:01:40.880 --> 00:01:45.520
The supply curve shows the number of goods or&nbsp;
services sellers are willing to provide at a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:45.520 --> 00:01:50.000
given price. It curves upward because&nbsp;
the higher the price, the more supply.

00:01:52.400 --> 00:01:59.200
As we already know, at $1 the people on&nbsp;
the market demand 150 cups a day. However,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:59.200 --> 00:02:04.880
the owner already pays $1.20 for the beans per&nbsp;
cup. Which means, he can’t sell a cup for just $1.

00:02:07.360 --> 00:02:12.000
If the price were to be $12 per cup, the&nbsp;
owner could hire two additional staff,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:12.000 --> 00:02:17.920
while supplying up to 150 cups. Unfortunately,&nbsp;
only 10 customers would buy at that price.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:17.920 --> 00:02:21.840
And that’s not enough to cover&nbsp;
the costs of the business.

00:02:22.640 --> 00:02:28.960
The sweet spot happens to be $3. At this&nbsp;
cost, the owner can hire one employee and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:28.960 --> 00:02:32.400
happily supply the 100 cups a day&nbsp;
that are demanded at that price.

00:02:34.880 --> 00:02:37.680
Supply and demand on one graph look like this:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:38.240 --> 00:02:44.800
At $12 the owner can supply 150 cups. But only 10&nbsp;
people would buy a cup of coffee at that price.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:45.360 --> 00:02:49.840
At 1 dollar he can’t supply any. But&nbsp;
demand would be at 150 cups of coffee.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:50.480 --> 00:02:56.080
The two curves meet at $3 and 100 cups.&nbsp;
Let’s now assume circumstances change.

00:02:58.400 --> 00:03:01.520
The summer was very hot, and&nbsp;
there was only a little rain,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:01.520 --> 00:03:07.680
which left many coffee bean crops destroyed.&nbsp;
The poor harvest yielded a low supply of beans.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:07.680 --> 00:03:13.120
As a result, more traders bid for the limited&nbsp;
amount of beans, leading to an increase of price.

00:03:15.840 --> 00:03:20.720
The shop owner now buys the beans from&nbsp;
the farmer at $2 for the beans per cup.&nbsp;

00:03:21.280 --> 00:03:26.400
Can he still sell at $3? He tries&nbsp;
and decides to let go of his employee&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:26.400 --> 00:03:28.560
and begins serving coffee all by himself.

00:03:30.560 --> 00:03:34.160
But he cannot meet the demand of all&nbsp;
his customers. If the government were&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:34.160 --> 00:03:38.320
to force him to keep the price fixed, as&nbsp;
some do through so-called price controls,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:38.320 --> 00:03:40.240
queuing would be part of everyone’s life.

00:03:42.160 --> 00:03:46.480
To reduce the wait times for his clients and&nbsp;
make up for the increased cost of the beans,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:46.480 --> 00:03:49.520
the owner decides to increase&nbsp;
the price of the coffee to $4.5.

00:03:53.120 --> 00:03:59.520
The supply curve now shifts to the left&nbsp;
and at $4.5, the shop will sell 70 cups&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:59.520 --> 00:04:04.560
of coffee per day. Typically, the higher the&nbsp;
selling price of a good, the greater number of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:04.560 --> 00:04:09.040
people willing to supply it. This means that&nbsp;
there will likely be more competition soon.

00:04:11.440 --> 00:04:15.120
In real life and within a free&nbsp;
market, if you drink coffee at $4.50,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:16.400 --> 00:04:20.080
it’s not the basis of an economic&nbsp;
calculation, but the result of an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:20.080 --> 00:04:25.280
endless dance of trial and error between sellers&nbsp;
and buyers that lead them to reach an unspoken&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:25.280 --> 00:04:31.120
agreement about what works best for both. This&nbsp;
phenomenon is referred to as emergent order.

00:04:33.360 --> 00:04:34.720
Before we summarize,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:34.720 --> 00:04:39.200
please note that economists differentiate&nbsp;
between “quantity demanded” and “demand”.

00:04:39.920 --> 00:04:43.920
A change in the “quantity demanded”&nbsp;
happens due to a change in price,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:43.920 --> 00:04:48.240
like we explored when the price dropped&nbsp;
and the quantity demanded increased.

00:04:48.800 --> 00:04:53.280
A “change in demand” happens when the&nbsp;
environment changes. Let’s imagine&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:53.280 --> 00:04:58.000
that many people in the market become more&nbsp;
health-conscious and think that coffee is bad.&nbsp;

00:04:58.000 --> 00:05:02.000
If that happens, the entire&nbsp;
demand curve shifts downward,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:02.000 --> 00:05:05.920
and at $3, not a hundred buy coffee but just 40.

00:05:08.320 --> 00:05:12.720
Let’s summarize: Supply is the&nbsp;
amount provided at each price&nbsp;

00:05:12.720 --> 00:05:18.640
Demand is the amount purchased at each price
The Final Price is the result of an emergent order

00:05:19.280 --> 00:05:24.240
If you are up for it, we will now ask you to&nbsp;
help us understand today’s price of coffee.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:24.240 --> 00:05:26.080
Post your thoughts in the comments below!

00:05:28.400 --> 00:05:34.000
Search for a Coffee Price Chart and you’ll find a&nbsp;
graph showing the price of coffee in US Dollars.

00:05:34.000 --> 00:05:38.320
Then tell us: What's the price&nbsp;
of one kilogram of coffee today?&nbsp;

00:05:38.320 --> 00:05:51.840
What do you think are the reasons that have&nbsp;
caused the price to fluctuate in the past 5 years?

